de  those i don't think those have to do  with repair i think those are more  likely a synthesis here or something to  do with the  long ssdna construction that had a  mistake in it and our  our pup just happened to use that as a  repair template  but the good news is that we did have  some pups these ones start in green that  were precise and they had exact  modification with no other errors or no  uh mistakes in the repair  again another thing that is good about  ngf but a limitation  is that we could not sequence through  the whole thing we could only sequence  through the business end  and since i talked to you about these  synthesizers it's really important to  sequence through the whole  insert before before going forward to  breeding this animal  all right just a few more slides um i  wanted to talk to you about this on  target activity limitation of pcr bias  so i mentioned this  pcr bias is seen easily and whenever  we're dealing with animals because we  know that in the f1 generation  mom can only give one allele and dad can  only give one allele so we know that it  should be messy  so this example we did embryo injections  found a pup that had a minus 33 base  pair deletion that was our f-zero  um then he was actually mosaic so he had  you know more than just two alleles but  he can only pass on one whenever you  made him to uh  or maybe i got remember boy made them to  a wild type black six  and so we know that the allylic ratio  and f1 generation should be 50 50 of  whatever we have  but when you look at the ngs data you  can see the pup 31 the very top one  had a minus 33 3 in bell at 61 percent  and my  uh and then wild type from the black  fixed parent at 36  so that larger deletion made the  amplicon about 10  smaller so the the pcr the polymerase  preferentially amplifies the smaller  endo so be aware of that whenever you're  doing any pcr-based assay whether it's  ngs  or or some ida or tighter eyes  from there and then i  talked about amplicon size and how  you're limited to really about a  thousand base pairs you can get kind of  creative with us and put more things  into your ngs run  one way we do this is whenever we use  double stranded dna which is what we  typically use for creating cell lines  we use a junction pcrs like i described  earlier but we have  in the double strand of dna we typically  use about 800 base pair homology arms  which makes this five prime junction and  the three prime junction amplicon about  900 base pairs in length  which is too big for ngs so what we do  is we'll do  actually three pcrs so we do the first  pcr which we call the long junction  then we take that pcr and we do another  pcr which we call the short junction  and then we do our indexing pcr so in  this case our short junctions  you should only give the amplicon if you  have the target integration and our  short junctions will be 400 base pairs  which we can sequence  so in this case you'll only get um reads  from the wells that have  the the the junction where you actually  have the long junction  and so you can do this since our output  is in excel you can either create  something in python which is what we did  or you can do something in excel we  compare how many pups had the five prime  positive how many pups had the  three prime positive or cell lines  clones and you can actually figure out  which ones have  uh both the five prime and three prime  junctions to know which ones to narrow  down fairly quickly  and so in this example 62 percent of the  clones that had the five prime  correct junction also had the three  prime correct junction  and then i also mentioned earlier uh  that there's a high barrier to entry for  ngs we hope that christy has helped with  the  you should not be intimidated by it  crispy again was written  by a guy who just learned how to do  python coding for this particular  application so it's super simple  um and then the cost is the other thing  that's barrier so what we've done to  make  ngs cost effective and actually cheaper  than finger sequencing in our case  um are several things and i just wanted  to share some tips  one is the more samples the better so we  sequence anything we can sequence we  throw into our weekly sample so we  sequence guide on a plasmas we take  those pools we sequence clones we do  junctions deletions translocations we  even do spr profiling to identify cell  identity  um so you can put anything you think you  put in there you should put in there  because  the more the better you can also pool  samples so we basically pull the samples  and we only do  one run a week so we basically take all  the samples that we can get all over  think jude  we run one week one run a week so that  we can make get the most bang for our  buck  you can also consider trying a spike in  program so if you don't need a lot of  reads but several other labs are  interested in getting some reads  um just the reagents cost from illumina  for the my sequin is about four hundred  dollars and you get a million reads  the my seat uh the sorry that was the  nano run the full run you get 15 million  reads and it's only 1200  for the reagents so you get 15 times the  number of reads and it's only three  times the cost so if you can get the  more rate more people that you can get  doing sequencing or the more samples  that you have  the cheaper it becomes we use the two  two-step pcr because it's generally more  effective versus like a true seeker you  have to use specific  primers um and then we try to make our  our setup as quick  and easy as possible so we do robotics  when possible on the pcr we use crude  lysis for the genomic dna and we use a  multi-drop to just fast dispense the  reagents  and so my last two slides are really  just going to kind of sum up this  notion that nds can actually be cheaper  than singer sequencing so hopefully it's  not going to be a barrier for you to  enter into this  spot so i mentioned that a mysq  full run is twelve hundred dollars for  the reagents i get that there's a  machine and the service contract also  involved but just the reagents  are twelve hundred dollars um you get  about fifteen million reads  typically if you do a thousand x cup  which is overkill for most of the things  that we are doing in our lab  um you could do 15 000 samples and that  will basically be  uh eight cents or eight cents a sample  so it's much cheaper than sanger  sequencing even if you add in  the cost of the service agreement and  the labor involved in  hiring somebody to help would run this  let's say you don't need 10 million  or 15 million reads you can do a nano  kit which is about 400  you get about a million reads if you  want a thousand x coverage you can do a  thousand samples  and this is only 40 cents a sample so  still again cheaper than zinger  sequencing and you get a lot of  information  so in conclusion i hope i've shown you  basically three main things  one ngs is an excellent on-target assay  for crispr activity  it's also good for off-target activity  but we use it really to figure out do we  have the modification that we want  also crispy is this python-based program  that is freely available on github and  it allows even novice users to  interrogate their data completely like  you can really dig down and figure out  what was the cell doing what did the  cell do in my and myself it's not a  black box that's really hard to  understand it's very simple and and  again i've trained a lot of scientists  on how to use this and interpret it  and it's very simple and then finally  ngs is a high throughput method for  quickly cost effectively and  quantitatively measuring your editing  outcomes  and then i'm going to leave you with  thinking the people who did all the work  um so i've been  i basically started the center uh the  geic at washington university  uh monica setman out was the one that  led the survey of the validation assays  with the help of  patrick connolly sam peters and colin  florian i also  mentioned patrick several times because  he was the one that developed the the  crispy python program and then really  we're very much a team  so everybody in both organizations was  involved in  and and these the data that i presented  today and then also the things the  funding  and i'll leave you uh with a picture of  the folks in my lab now  um who are working diligently every day  and then i'll take any questions that we  might have  and thank you dr miller for that  informative presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar  and if you have any questions you'd like  to ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen so let's get started  dr miller we already have some great  questions  coming in our first audience member says  thank you for your helpful presentation  based on your experience for knocking  out a bacterial virulence gene  that contains both the conserved and  hypervariable regions which  part is better for targeting  this is a question that i uh probably  cannot answer  because we haven't done that um i i  guess i assume that they're  talking well i'm not exactly sure what  they're specifically asking  um but typically whenever you're  knocking out a gene i mean you could  definitely delete the whole  thing like the two guy approach um if  you're worried about it  um but but i would say most of the time  if you get an indel in there it'll it'll  it'll mess it up it'll cause it not to  be expressed um  but i would say if you're really worried  about it just do a deletion where you  delete the whole thing out  um if it's going to be small most most  genes are 1200 base pairs or less if  you're looking at the cdna  um so i would say deletions of 1200  bases are fairly efficient so that would  be no problem at all  wonderful thank you we have time for one  more question  is crispy available for download and if  so  how can i download the program  yeah